speechtext:
I understand that the present campaign has been conducted by an honorary committee of citizens; I do not think it has been conducted by the official air raid committee. Here is the reply I made:
This budget contemplates:
(2)	that the dominion government shall pay for light, heat and janitor services to the extent of more than $1,000 a month, in the Vancouver city hall and other civic premises;
(3)	that the dominion government shall pay the salaries of at least nine permanent civic employees of the city of Vancouver;
(4)	that the dominion government shall pay for the expense of operating a police car;
(5)	that we shall pay the salaries of some ten or eleven stenographers;
(6)	that we shall pay some $2,200 for a control room, already established in the city hall. This sum is indicated as a non-recurring commitment already made, and I am advised by my departmental officers that it consists of a room in the city hall containing some plain furniture and maps, for which separate items are submitted;
(7)	this budget contains the following extraordinary items which the dominion government is asked to approve:
Miscellaneous $105.20 monthly,
Miscellaneous $35.70 monthly,
Miscellaneous $6.10 monthly;
(8)	the budget includes several hundred dollars for telephone services upon which my officers are unable to comment without more definite information, either by way of your recommendation or personal inspection by our own representative in British Columbia. In
War Appropriation

any event, we have had no such item from any other city, although there are several in which efficient precautions have been maintained for two and a half years.
With deep regret and a full sense of my responsibility, both as a minister and as a representative of Vancouver, I must inform you that I cannot approve this budget.
Could any minister of the crown support items for which no details are given?
